<section xml:id="sec-ch04-04-DoubleIntegralsGeneralRegionsPart1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Double Integrals in Cartesian Coordinates</title>
  <subtitle>General Regions</subtitle>

  <note>
    <p>
      In this section we will do the same thing we did in the previous: We
      will find the net volume bounded by a surface <m>f(x, y)</m> and the
      <m>x y</m>-plane, restricted to a region <m>R</m> in its domain.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    However, we will now consider regions in the plane whose upper and
    lower bounds are given by curves (rather than straight lines as with
    rectangles), as shown below:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-region-between-curves">
    <caption>The area between the function f and the function g</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-01(1). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-region-between-curves" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-01-1.png">
          <shortdescription>The area between the function f and the function g</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The area between the function <m>f</m> and the function <m>g</m></p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      The process to find the net volume is exactly the same as before. We
      still partition the region <m>R</m> into rectangles, although at
      first they won't fit inside very nicely.
    </p>
  </note>

  <!--\begin{figure}[H]
  \includegraphics[max width=0.5\textwidth, center]{2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-01}
  \caption*{
  Portion of $R$ covered with rectangles (completely in $R$ ) in orange}
  \end{figure}-->

  <p>
    As we increase the number of rectangles, we will get a better fit. When
    we take the number of rectangles to approach infinity, we theoretically
    cover the whole region <m>R</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-rectangles-inside-region">
    <caption>Portion of region R covered with rectangles that lie completely inside R.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-02(1). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-rectangles-inside-region" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-02-1.png">
          <shortdescription>Portion of region R covered with rectangles that lie completely inside R.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Portion of region <m>R</m> covered with rectangles that lie completely inside <m>R</m>.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Since the height of each rectangular prism is still given by
    <m>f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m> (where <m>\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m>
    is a point in the <m>k</m>-th rectangle) and the area of each rectangle
    is still <m>A_{k}=\Delta x_{k} \Delta y_{k}</m>, we get the same
    formula for the net volume:
    <md>
      V=\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A_{k}=\iint_{R} f(x, y) d A
    </md>
  </p>

  <note>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals over General Regions</title>

    <p>
      As before, we will partition the net volume into cross sections, with
      net area <m>A(x)</m>, parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis (keep <m>x</m> as
      a constant); the difference is the upper and lower bounds of the
      region <m>R</m> are now functions <m>g(x)</m> and <m>h(x)</m> (instead
      of constants)
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-cross-section-1">
    <caption>Left: a cross section for a given value of x; Right: the net area of the cross section</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-02. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-cross-section-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-02.png">
          <shortdescription>Left: a cross section for a given value of x; Right: the net area of the cross section</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Left: a cross section for a given value of <m>x</m>; Right: the net area of the cross section</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-cross-section-2">
    <caption>Left: a cross section with x equals c 1; Right: the net area of the cross section</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-03(1). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-cross-section-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-03-1.png">
          <shortdescription>Left: a cross section with x equals c 1; Right: the net area of the cross section</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Left: a cross section with <m>x=c_1</m>; Right: the net area of the cross section</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we want to integrate the function <m>f(x, y)=3 x+ y</m> on
        the region <m>R</m> bounded above by <m>y=x</m> and below by
        <m>y=\frac{1}{2} x</m>, for <m>0 \leq x \leq 2</m>, as shown below.
        Find the net area of the cross section corresponding to <m>x=1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-ex-17-1">
        <caption>The area of the cross section</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-03. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-ex-17-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-03.png">
              <shortdescription>The area of the cross section</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The area of the cross section</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        At <m>x=1</m> the region <m>R</m> has <m>y</m> ranging from the
        lower bound <m>y=\frac{1}{2}x</m> to the upper bound <m>y=x</m>.
        Substituting <m>x=1</m> gives <m>y</m> ranging from
        <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> to <m>1</m>. The net area of the cross section
        is the integral of <m>f(1, y) = 3 + y</m> with respect to <m>y</m>
        over this interval:
        <md>
          A(1) = \int_{1/2}^{1} (3 + y)\, dy = \left. 3y + \frac{y^2}{2} \right|_{1/2}^{1} = \left(3 + \frac{1}{2}\right) - \left(\frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{8}\right) = \frac{7}{2} - \frac{13}{8} = \frac{28}{8} - \frac{13}{8} = \frac{15}{8}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    More generally, to find the net area of an arbitrary cross section, we
    integrate <m>f(x, y)</m> with respect to <m>y</m> with the bounds of
    integration given by the upper and lower bounds of <m>R</m>. That is,
    <md>
      A(x)=\int_{g(x)}^{h(x)} f(x, y) d y
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Consequently, the net volume of the whole region is given by
    <md>
      V=\int_{a}^{b} A(x) d x
    </md>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals as Iterated Integrals</title>

    <p>
      To integrate a continuous function <m>f(x, y)</m> over a region
      <m>R</m> bounded by continuous functions <m>y=g(x)</m> and
      <m>y=h(x)</m> from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>, use the formula
      <md>
        \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{a}^{b} \int_{g(x)}^{h(x)} f(x, y) d y d x
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      To find the bounds <m>y=g(x)</m> and <m>y=h(x)</m> when integrating
      in the order <m>d y d x</m> :
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Draw a sketch of the region <m>R</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Draw vertical lines from bottom to top.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the vertical lines (from step 2) intersect
            first (as they enter the region) is the lower bound
            <m>y=g(x)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the vertical lines (from step 2) intersect
            second (as they leave the region) is the upper bound
            <m>y=h(x)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\iint_{R} 2 x y d A</m> where <m>R</m> is the region
        bounded by the parabolas <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y=x^{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-ex-17-2">
        <caption>The surface of the function f and the net volume</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-04. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-ex-17-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-04.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface of the function f and the net volume</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface of the function <m>f</m> and the net volume</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The surface <m>f(x, y)= xy</m> in blue and the net volume in orange
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        First we find where the two curves intersect to determine the
        <m>x</m>-bounds. Setting <m>\sqrt{x} = x^2</m> gives <m>x = x^4</m>,
        hence <m>x(x^3-1)=0</m>, so <m>x=0</m> or <m>x=1</m>. The curves
        meet at <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(1,1)</m>, giving <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m>.
        For a fixed <m>x\in[0,1]</m> a vertical line enters the region at
        <m>y=x^2</m> (the lower boundary, since <m>x^2\leq\sqrt{x}</m> on
        <m>[0,1]</m>) and exits at <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> (the upper boundary).
        Hence
        <md>
          \iint_{R} 2xy\, dA = \int_{0}^{1} \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} 2xy\, dy\, dx
        </md>.
        Evaluating the inner integral with <m>x</m> treated as a constant:
        <md>
          \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} 2xy\, dy = \left. xy^2 \right|_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} = x(\sqrt{x})^2 - x(x^2)^2 = x^2 - x^5
        </md>.
        Then integrating with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{1} (x^2 - x^5)\, dx = \left. \frac{x^3}{3} - \frac{x^6}{6} \right|_{0}^{1} = \frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{6} = \frac{1}{6}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the (net) volume of the prism whose base is the triangle in
        the <m>x y</m>-plane bounded by <m>x</m>-axis and the lines
        <m>y=x</m> and <m>x=1</m> and whose top lies on the plane
        <m>z=6-3 x^2-2y</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-ex-17-3">
        <caption>The surface of the function f and the net volume</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-07. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-ex-17-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-07.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface of the function f and the net volume</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface of the function <m>f</m> and the net volume</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The surface <m>f(x, y)=6-3 x^2-2y</m> in blue and the net volume in
        orange
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The three boundary lines <m>y=0</m>, <m>y=x</m>, and <m>x=1</m> meet
        at <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>, and <m>(1,1)</m>. For a fixed
        <m>x\in[0,1]</m> a vertical line enters the triangle at <m>y=0</m>
        and exits at <m>y=x</m>, giving the bounds <m>0\leq y\leq x</m> and
        <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m>. Hence
        <md>
          V = \int_{0}^{1} \int_{0}^{x} (6 - 3x^2 - 2y)\, dy\, dx
        </md>.
        Evaluating the inner integral:
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{x} (6 - 3x^2 - 2y)\, dy = \left. (6 - 3x^2)y - y^2 \right|_{0}^{x} = (6 - 3x^2)x - x^2 = 6x - 3x^3 - x^2
        </md>.
        Then integrating with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{1} (6x - 3x^3 - x^2)\, dx = \left. 3x^2 - \frac{3x^4}{4} - \frac{x^3}{3} \right|_{0}^{1} = 3 - \frac{3}{4} - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{36}{12} - \frac{9}{12} - \frac{4}{12} = \frac{23}{12}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Sometimes the region of integration <m>R</m> is more easily described
      as being bounded to the left and right instead of below and above. In
      this case, the boundaries are functions of <m>\mathbf{y}</m>, as shown
      below:
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-region-left-right">
      <caption>The area between the function f and the function g</caption>

      <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-08. -->
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-region-left-right" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-08.png">
            <shortdescription>The area between the function f and the function g</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The area between the function <m>f</m> and the function <m>g</m></p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <p>
    In this case, we use cross sections parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis
    (treat <m>y</m> as a constant). Now, we have that the net area of the
    cross sections is given by
    <md>
      A(y)=\int_{g(y)}^{h(y)} f(x, y) d x
    </md>
    and the net volume is given by
    <md>
      V=\int_{c}^{d} A(y) d y
    </md>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals as Iterated Integrals</title>

    <p>
      To integrate a continuous function <m>f(x, y)</m> over a region
      <m>R</m> bounded by continuous functions <m>x=g(y)</m> and
      <m>x=h(y)</m> from <m>y=c</m> to <m>y=d</m>, use the formula
      <md>
        \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{c}^{d} \int_{g(y)}^{h(y)} f(x, y) d x d y
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      To find the bounds <m>x=g(y)</m> and <m>x=h(y)</m> when integrating
      in the order <m>d x d y</m> :
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Draw a sketch of the region <m>R</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Draw horizontal lines from left to right.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the horizontal lines intersect first (as
            they enter the region) is the lower (leftmost) bound
            <m>x=g(y)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the horizontal lines intersects second (as
            they leave the region) is the upper (rightmost) bound
            <m>x=h(y)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the (net) volume of the prism whose base is the triangle in
        the <m>x y</m>-plane bounded by <m>x</m>-axis and the lines
        <m>y=x</m> and <m>x=1</m> and whose top lies on the plane
        <m>z=6-3 x^2-2y</m> (in the order <m>d x d y</m> ).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The triangle has vertices <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>, and
        <m>(1,1)</m>. For a fixed <m>y\in[0,1]</m> a horizontal line enters
        the triangle at <m>x=y</m> (from the line <m>y=x</m> solved for
        <m>x</m>) and exits at <m>x=1</m>, giving the bounds
        <m>y\leq x\leq 1</m> and <m>0\leq y\leq 1</m>. Hence
        <md>
          V = \int_{0}^{1} \int_{y}^{1} (6 - 3x^2 - 2y)\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
        Evaluating the inner integral:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_{y}^{1} (6 - 3x^2 - 2y)\, dx = \left. 6x - x^3 - 2yx \right|_{y}^{1} = (6 - 1 - 2y) - (6y - y^3 - 2y^2)</mrow>
          <mrow>= y^3 + 2y^2 - 8y + 5</mrow>
        </md>.
        Then integrating with respect to <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{1} (y^3 + 2y^2 - 8y + 5)\, dy = \left. \frac{y^4}{4} + \frac{2y^3}{3} - 4y^2 + 5y \right|_{0}^{1} = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{2}{3} - 4 + 5 = \frac{3}{12} + \frac{8}{12} + \frac{12}{12} = \frac{23}{12}
        </md>,
        which matches the result from <xref ref="example-ch04-04-ex-17-3"/>,
        as expected.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Partitioning Regions of Integration</title>

    <p>
      Suppose we are evaluating an iterated integral using the order
      <m>d y d x</m> (cross sections parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis). If the
      lower and upper bounds of <m>R</m> change throughout the region, you
      must partition <m>R</m> every time such a change occurs and integrate
      over each piece separately,
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the order <m>d y d x</m>, set up (but do not evaluate) an
        iterated integral(s) that give the (net) volume of the wedgelike
        solid that lies beneath the surface <m>z=1-x-y</m> and above the
        region <m>R</m> bounded by the curve <m>y= \frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}</m>,
        the line <m>y= x-1</m>, and the <m>x</m>-axis.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-ex-17-5">
        <caption>The region R and its partition into R 1 and R 2</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-10. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-ex-17-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-10.png">
              <shortdescription>The region R and its partition into R 1 and R 2</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The region <m>R</m> and its partition into <m>R_{1}</m> and <m>R_{2}</m></p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The region <m>R</m> and its partition into <m>R_{1}</m> and
        <m>R_{2}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first find the intersection points of the three boundary curves.
        Setting <m>\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}=x-1</m> and letting <m>u=\sqrt{x}</m>
        gives <m>\frac{3}{2}u=u^2-1</m>, i.e. <m>2u^2-3u-2=0</m>, which
        factors as <m>(2u+1)(u-2)=0</m>. Since <m>u\geq 0</m> we get
        <m>u=2</m>, so <m>x=4</m> and <m>y=3</m>. The curve meets the
        <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=0</m>) at <m>(0,0)</m> and the line meets the
        <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=0</m>) at <m>(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a fixed <m>x</m>, a vertical line crosses the region differently
        depending on <m>x</m>: for <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m> the lower boundary is
        the line <m>y=0</m> (<m>x</m>-axis) and the upper boundary is the
        parabola <m>y=\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}</m>, while for <m>1\leq x\leq 4</m>
        the lower boundary is the line <m>y=x-1</m> and the upper boundary
        remains <m>y=\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}</m>. Hence
        <md>
          V = \int_0^1 \int_{0}^{\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}} 1- x - y\, dy\, dx + \int_1^{4} \int_{x-1}^{\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}} 1-x-y\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Similarly, when integrating in the order <m>d x d y</m>, if the
      leftmost or rightmost bounds change, you need to partition the region
      <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the order <m>d x d y</m>, set up (but do not evaluate) an
        iterated integral(s) that gives the net volume bounded by
        <m>f(x, y)=2</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane restricted to the region
        <m>R</m> bounded by <m>x=0, y=2^{x}</m>, and <m>y=4-2 x</m>. You can
        assume the two curves intersect only at the point (1, 2)
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The exponential <m>y=2^x</m> passes through <m>(0,1)</m> and the
        line <m>y=4-2x</m> has <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,4)</m> and meets the
        exponential at <m>(1,2)</m>, as mentioned above. The <m>y</m>-axis
        (<m>x=0</m>) meets the exponential at <m>(0,1)</m> and the line at
        <m>(0,4)</m>. For a fixed <m>y</m>, a horizontal line crosses the
        region differently depending on <m>y</m>: for <m>1\leq y\leq 2</m>
        the left boundary is the exponential <m>x=\log_2 y</m> and the right
        boundary is the line <m>x=\frac{4-y}{2}</m>, while for
        <m>2\leq y\leq 4</m> the left boundary is the <m>y</m>-axis
        <m>x=0</m> and the right boundary remains <m>x=\frac{4-y}{2}</m>.
        Hence
        <md>
          V = \int_1^2 \int_{\log_2 y}^{\frac{4-y}{2}} 2\, dx\, dy + \int_2^4 \int_0^{\frac{4-y}{2}} 2\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Sometimes, changing the order of integration simplifies the amount of
      work when integrating.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-04-ex-17-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) an iterated integral(s) that gives the
        net volume from <xref ref="example-ch04-04-ex-17-5"/> Use the order
        <m>d x d y</m>. (Beneath the surface <m>z=1-x-y</m> and above the
        region <m>R</m> bounded by the curve <m>y= \frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}</m>,
        the line <m>y= x-1</m>, and the <m>x</m>-axis.)
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-04-ex-17-7">
        <caption>The domain of integration</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-13. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-04-ex-17-7" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_21_8b2f41f086d94a698980g-13.png">
              <shortdescription>The domain of integration</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The domain of integration</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first find the intersection points of the three boundary curves.
        Setting <m>\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}=x-1</m> and letting <m>u=\sqrt{x}</m>
        gives <m>\frac{3}{2}u=u^2-1</m>, i.e. <m>2u^2-3u-2=0</m>, which
        factors as <m>(2u+1)(u-2)=0</m>. Since <m>u\geq 0</m> we do not
        consider the case <m>u=-\frac{1}{2}</m>, we only get <m>u=2</m>, so
        <m>x=4</m> and <m>y=3</m>. The curve meets the <m>x</m>-axis
        (<m>y=0</m>) at <m>(0,0)</m> and the line meets the <m>x</m>-axis
        (<m>y=0</m>) at <m>(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Solving the boundary curves for <m>x</m> in terms of <m>y</m>: from
        <m>y=\frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x}</m> we get <m>x=\frac{4y^2}{9}</m>, and from
        <m>y=x-1</m> we get <m>x=y+1</m>. For a fixed <m>y\in[0,3]</m> a
        horizontal line enters the region at the curve
        <m>x=\frac{4y^2}{9}</m> and exits at the line <m>x=y+1</m>, with no
        change in which boundary is which throughout the entire interval.
        Hence only a single integral is needed:
        <md>
          V = \int_0^3 \int_{\frac{4y^2}{9}}^{y+1} (1 - x - y)\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
